To fix the issue of the hover event firing in your responsive navigation, you can use jQuery's `mouseenter` and `mouseleave` methods instead of `hover`. These methods allow you to attach specific event handlers for mouse entering and leaving the navigation item, providing more control over when the menu should open and close.

Here's an example of how you can update your code:

```javascript
$(document).ready(function () {

  var $nav = $('.nav-item');

  $nav.on('mouseenter', function () {
    $('.nav-menu').show();
  }).on('mouseleave', function () {
    $('.nav-menu').hide();
  });

  var $menu = $('.nav-menu');

  $menu.on('click', function (event) {
    event.preventDefault(); // Prevents the default behavior of the anchor tag
    $('.nav-menu').toggle();
  });

  $menu.on('click', 'li a', function (event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    var $subMenu = $(this).closest('.nav-item').find('.nav-submenu');

    if ($subMenu.is(':visible')) {
      $subMenu.hide();
    } else {
      $subMenu.show();
    }
  });

  $(window).on('resize', function () {
    var $nav = $('.nav-item');
    if ($(window).width() > 768) {
      $nav.hide();
    } else {
      $nav.show();
    }
  });

});
```

In this updated code, I used `mouseenter` and `mouseleave` instead of `hover`. I also added a `click` event handler for the menu item, which prevents the default behavior of the anchor tag. This way, the navigation menu can only be opened and closed by clicking on the menu item.

Additionally, I added an event listener for the window `resize` event, which hides the navigation menu if the window width is less than 768px (or any other value you choose). This ensures that the navigation menu is only visible on desktop views.

Remember to update your HTML structure to reflect the changes in the code. For example, you can remove the `hover` event from the anchor tags and add the `mouseenter` and `mouseleave` event handlers directly to the `li` elements representing the navigation items.

